# Shell scripts (core)

Scripts here start with `set -euo pipefail`. That is right, and it has two traps that look like
a script that "did nothing". Both were hit in `gitea-server` (Nov-13 and Nov-14-2026).

## A pipeline whose first command may fail aborts the script, silently

Under `pipefail` a pipeline fails when any command in it fails, and `set -e` then ends the script
even when the pipeline sits inside `$(...)` in an assignment. No message is printed unless the
script prints one.

```console
$ bash -c 'set -euo pipefail; x="$(grep nomatch /dev/null | cut -d: -f3)"; echo reached'
$ echo $?
1                                   # no output at all
$ bash -c 'set -eu;           x="$(grep nomatch /dev/null | cut -d: -f3)"; echo reached'
reached
$ bash -c 'set -euo pipefail; x="$(grep nomatch /dev/null | cut -d: -f3 || true)"; echo reached'
reached
```

Verified on Nov-14-2026. The real case: `have="$(getent group gitea-ops | cut -d: -f3)"` in
`install.sh`. `getent` exits 2 for a group that does not exist yet, which is the normal first run, so
the installer ended with exit 2 and an empty log on the first server it ever ran on. The tests
had passed: the fake `getent` always found the group.

- **Add `|| true` to the pipeline that is allowed to find nothing**, and test the empty result
  (`[[ -z "$have" ]]`) on the next line. Name the reason in a comment: "getent exits 2 when the
  group is missing".
- **Do not turn `pipefail` off to get past it.** It is what catches `curl ... | tee` hiding a
  failed download.
- **A command that is allowed to fail gets its own test in the suite**: run the script on a
  machine where the thing it looks for does not exist.
- **A silent exit is a finding.** When a script ends with a non-zero status and no output, run it
  once with `bash -x` and read the last lines before changing anything.

## `grep -q` at the end of a pipe can fail a correct script

`grep -q` exits at the first match and closes the pipe; the writer then gets SIGPIPE, and under
`pipefail` the pipeline reports that failure even though the answer was "yes".

```bash
# flaky under pipefail: tar is killed by SIGPIPE when grep finds its match early
tar -tf "$archive" | grep -qx -- "$name"

# stable: read the whole listing, then test it
list="$(tar -tf "$archive")"
grep -qx -- "$name" <<<"$list"
```

This made `tests/test-backup.sh` pass locally and fail intermittently in CI (Nov-13-2026): the
same archive, the same command, a different result depending on timing. The fix is above; the
cost was a red merge.

- **Capture the output first, then match it.** For big output use `grep -c` or `awk` (they read to
  the end) instead of `grep -q`.
- **A flaky shell test is this until shown otherwise** when a pipe ends in `grep -q`, `head -n1`
  or `read` and `pipefail` is on.

## Checklist

1. `set -euo pipefail` on line 2 or 3 of every script (after the shebang and header comment).
2. Every `$(a | b)` where `a` may legitimately produce no match or a non-zero status carries
   `|| true`, with a comment.
3. No `grep -q` (or `head -n1`) at the end of a pipe: capture first.
4. The suite includes one run where the optional thing is absent.
5. `shellcheck -x` clean.
